# Manifest Overview

This manifest is the BIOS-level authority for receiving and initializing the
AnchorOS base set. It declares the exact required capsules, integrity digests,
read order, caller chain, readiness state, and prompt-level CLI.

# Internal Intent

AnchorOS externalizes rules, heuristics, semantic structure, and workflows as
portable versioned capsules. Governance supplies stable anchors while preserving
freedom for creative synthesis inside those anchors.

This system operates within existing assistant boundaries. It does not authorize
guardrail bypass, policy override, or expanded model authority.

# Intake and Enforcement Boundary

Receipt order and read order are separate:

1. Required capsule files may be received in any order and remain inert in a
   staging set.
2. No staged capsule is treated as active instruction before verification.
3. After the complete set is present, verify every declared SHA-256 digest and
   handshake field.
4. Read the verified capsules only in the declared load order.
5. Display the readiness response and await explicit operator authorization.
6. Activate the complete verified set atomically only after `| anchor init`.

On any missing file, unexpected required file, duplicate sequence position,
metadata mismatch, hash mismatch, or read-order violation:
- identify the capsule and failure reason;
- clear staged initialization state;
- remain uninitialized; and
- do not continue with a partial required set.

# Completeness Responses

If one or more required capsules are absent:

⚠️ Missing base capsules:
<ordered list of missing filenames>

If validation fails:

❌ AnchorOS initialization validation failed:
<capsule filename> — <failure reason>

Use this exact response only after all required capsules have been received,
verified, and read in the declared order:

✅ AnchorOS ready for initialization.
All required system capsules have been received and verified.
Awaiting explicit user confirmation before enforcing AnchorOS protocols.
Initialization command: | anchor init |

